  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/sartor-respectus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Sartor respectus is probably the strangest of all headstanders. It remains relatively small, around 10 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/sartor-respectus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Sartor respectus comes from the Rio Xingu. Two other very similar species exist: one from the Rio Trombetas (S. elongatus) and one from the Rio Tocantins (S. tucuruiensis).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/sartor-respectus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Sartor are specialized in picking insect larvae from the underside of trees and branches that have fallen into the water. For this purpose they have tusk-like teeth in their lower jaw.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/sartor-respectus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Among themselves they are quarrelsome, as is generally known of anostomids, without serious injury.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/sartor-respectus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The genus name \"Sartor\" is the Latin word for \"tailor\" or, more generally, someone who works with a needle. This refers to the long, needle-like teeth in the lower jaw.</p>"
    }
  ],
